  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2020 book from Peter Lang addresses cultural diversity in 21st-century workplaces, a topic aligned with the UN Sustainable Development Goal on reduced inequalities. Published in the established \"Travail et Soci\u00e9t\u00e9 / Work and Society\" series, the work appears in library collections but has not yet accumulated measurable citations in OpenAlex. Early scholarly uptake data remain limited, though the work's positioning within an active research series on labour and social policy suggests engagement within its specialist community.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Authored by Ariane Le Moing, Sa\u00efd Ouaked, and Christ\u00e8le Le Bihan\u2014scholars affiliated with research units specializing in human interactions and African studies\u2014this book examines how organizations navigate cultural diversity as a workplace reality. The work carries direct relevance to policy discourse on inequality reduction, scoring 0.54 on alignment with SDG 10 (Reduced Inequalities), reflecting its engagement with equity dimensions of labour and organizational life.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">The book is published within the \"Travail et Soci\u00e9t\u00e9 / Work and Society\" series (ISSN 1376-0955), a peer-reviewed platform at Peter Lang Verlag that has steadily documented scholarship on labour, employment policy, and social systems across Europe and beyond. Peer volumes in the series span trade union studies, pension policy reform, employment pathways, and workplace diversity policy, positioning this work within an established intellectual community focused on work-related social challenges.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Library holdings are recorded in WorldCat and Open Library, indicating presence in academic collections, though download metrics are not available from the current data record. No citations have accumulated in OpenAlex to date. This absence does not indicate lack of relevance; rather, it reflects the early visibility window for a specialized monograph in labour studies published in 2020. Citation patterns for books\u2014particularly those published in French and issued by university presses\u2014typically develop over years rather than months, and cross-citation between European language scholarship and English-indexed databases can be episodic.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">The work's contribution lies primarily in its engagement with a persistent organizational challenge: how institutions at the organizational and policy levels address cultural difference in employment contexts. Its publishers, co-authors' research profiles, and series placement together suggest that the book's primary audience and ongoing influence will be found within francophone labour studies, organizational sociology, and policy research communities rather than in immediate citation visibility across broad indexed databases.</p>",
